1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:01,583
The server is online.

2
00:00:01,933 --> 00:00:03,213
Why won't the page load?

3
00:00:03,563 --> 00:00:04,614
Follow the traffic.

4
00:00:04,964 --> 00:00:07,301
This network link has limited capacity.

5
00:00:07,651 --> 00:00:10,945
Normally, packets pass through and the service stays reachable.

6
00:00:11,295 --> 00:00:15,346
Now, many devices send attack traffic toward the same destination.

7
00:00:15,696 --> 00:00:18,152
Their separate paths converge on one shared link.

8
00:00:18,502 --> 00:00:20,138
Its capacity hasn't changed.

9
00:00:20,488 --> 00:00:22,810
More traffic arrives than the link can carry.

10
00:00:23,160 --> 00:00:24,365
Packets wait in a queue.

11
00:00:24,715 --> 00:00:27,424
When that queue fills, additional packets are dropped.

12
00:00:27,774 --> 00:00:30,253
Legitimate traffic shares this bottleneck too.

13
00:00:30,603 --> 00:00:34,614
A normal request may be delayed or lost before it reaches the server.

14
00:00:34,964 --> 00:00:37,948
The machine can still be running while users cannot reach it.

15
00:00:38,298 --> 00:00:42,382
That's one way a distributed denial of service takes a service offline.
